1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,603
Monday, the 27th of March, 1905.

2
00:00:03,603 --> 00:00:09,624
Half past eight in the morning, Detford High Street, in south-east London.

3
00:00:09,624 --> 00:00:18,330
A young shop assistant named William Jones arrives for work at Chapman's Oil and Colour Shop, at number 34.

4
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:20,923
The shop should already be open.

5
00:00:20,923 --> 00:00:24,806
Instead, the shutters are still up and the door is locked.

6
00:00:24,806 --> 00:00:25,677
Jones knocks.

7
00:00:25,677 --> 00:00:26,681
Nobody answers.

8
00:00:26,681 --> 00:00:30,096
Through the window, he can see chairs knocked over.

9
00:00:30,547 --> 00:00:39,902
Within days, Scotland Yard will have a single clue: a greasy smudge, about the size of a thumb, on the inside tray of a metal cash box.

10
00:00:39,902 --> 00:00:44,059
By the end of May, two young men will be dead because of it.

11
00:00:44,509 --> 00:00:50,297
No British jury had ever convicted anyone of murder on the strength of a fingerprint.

12
00:00:50,297 --> 00:00:53,498
The method was four years old at Scotland Yard.

13
00:00:53,498 --> 00:01:01,670
Its own champions knew that if it failed in a murder trial, in front of the newspapers, it might never be trusted again.

14
00:01:02,119 --> 00:01:03,891
This is Precinct Archive.

15
00:01:03,891 --> 00:01:13,456
This is the case of the Detford murders, and how one thumbprint on a cash box became a turning point in the history of British justice.

16
00:01:15,307 --> 00:01:24,210
Detford in 1905 was a working district on the south bank of the Thames, a few miles downriver from the City of London.

17
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:27,831
Dockyards, cattle markets, pubs and small shops.

18
00:01:27,831 --> 00:01:34,621
Detford High Street was its spine: a long, busy road of butchers, drapers and ironmongers.

19
00:01:35,072 --> 00:01:40,577
Chapman's Oil and Colour Shop sold paint, oils, brushes and household goods.

20
00:01:40,577 --> 00:01:45,213
It was owned by a man named George Chapman, who had other shops.

21
00:01:45,213 --> 00:01:47,966
Day to day, it was run by its manager.

22
00:01:48,415 --> 00:01:50,370
His name was Thomas Farrow.

23
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:57,028
He was 71 years old, and he lived in the flat above the shop with his wife, Ann, who was 65.

24
00:01:57,028 --> 00:02:00,285
They had looked after the business for years.

25
00:02:00,736 --> 00:02:05,170
There was one more habit in that shop that matters to this story.

26
00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:10,901
Every Monday morning, George Chapman came by to collect the previous week's takings.

27
00:02:10,901 --> 00:02:14,653
Until then, the money was kept upstairs, in a cash box.

28
00:02:15,104 --> 00:02:19,445
So on that Monday morning, William Jones knew something was wrong.

29
00:02:19,445 --> 00:02:24,774
He ran for help, and found a man named Louis Kidman, who worked in a shop nearby.

30
00:02:24,774 --> 00:02:27,998
Together they forced their way into the building.

31
00:02:28,448 --> 00:02:33,153
In the parlour behind the shop, they found Thomas Farrow on the floor.

32
00:02:33,153 --> 00:02:33,959
He was dead.

33
00:02:33,959 --> 00:02:36,245
He had been beaten about the head.

34
00:02:36,245 --> 00:02:39,875
Upstairs, in the bedroom, Ann Farrow was lying in bed.

35
00:02:39,875 --> 00:02:43,774
She was unconscious, also badly beaten, but she was alive.

36
00:02:44,223 --> 00:02:46,762
A doctor and the police were sent for.

37
00:02:46,762 --> 00:02:48,966
Ann Farrow was taken to hospital.

38
00:02:48,966 --> 00:02:54,110
If she woke up, she could tell the police exactly who had come into her home.

39
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,907
The police began to piece together what had happened.

40
00:02:57,907 --> 00:03:00,182
There was no sign of a forced entry.

41
00:03:00,182 --> 00:03:03,214
Both of the Farrows were in their night clothes.

42
00:03:03,214 --> 00:03:11,741
The likeliest explanation was that someone had knocked early, and that Mr Farrow, still half asleep, had come down and opened the door.

43
00:03:12,192 --> 00:03:13,986
He was struck almost at once.

44
00:03:13,986 --> 00:03:22,588
From the blood in different parts of the house, the police concluded that he had got up and tried to fight back, and had been struck again.

45
00:03:22,588 --> 00:03:27,168
The attackers had gone upstairs, found Mrs Farrow, and found the cash box.

46
00:03:27,168 --> 00:03:30,695
That is the police reconstruction, not a witness account.

47
00:03:30,695 --> 00:03:34,409
Nobody who was in that house that morning ever described it.

48
00:03:34,859 --> 00:03:37,208
Then the police found something else.

49
00:03:37,208 --> 00:03:43,242
On the floor lay two masks, made from the tops of black stockings, with holes cut for the eyes.

50
00:03:43,242 --> 00:03:44,766
Two masks meant two men.

51
00:03:44,766 --> 00:03:48,831
And a mask is worn by someone who thinks he might be recognised.

52
00:03:48,831 --> 00:03:53,911
That is an inference, not a fact from the file, but it shaped the whole inquiry.

53
00:03:53,911 --> 00:03:55,817
These were probably local men.

54
00:03:56,266 --> 00:04:00,688
And on the bedroom floor lay the cash box itself, open and empty.

55
00:04:00,688 --> 00:04:04,634
Accounts put the missing takings at about thirteen pounds.

56
00:04:04,634 --> 00:04:09,736
Two lives, for roughly what a working man might earn in a couple of months.

57
00:04:10,187 --> 00:04:18,566
At some point that morning, a police sergeant named Albert Atkinson saw the cash box lying where the doctor would have to step.

58
00:04:18,566 --> 00:04:21,800
He pushed it out of the way, with his bare hands.

59
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,099
In 1905, nobody would have thought twice about it.

60
00:04:25,099 --> 00:04:29,652
Hold on to that, because in a few weeks it would matter a great deal.

61
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:33,798
The case went straight to the top.

62
00:04:33,798 --> 00:04:47,445
Chief Inspector Frederick Fox was put in charge, and he was soon joined by Melville Mack-nawten, the Assistant Commissioner for crime, the head of the Criminal Investigation Department at Scotland Yard.

63
00:04:47,896 --> 00:04:49,990
Mack-nawten was not a scientist.

64
00:04:49,990 --> 00:04:59,610
But five years earlier he had sat on a Home Office committee, the Belper Committee, which had been asked how Britain should identify its criminals.

65
00:04:59,610 --> 00:05:02,883
It had recommended a new method over an older one.

66
00:05:02,883 --> 00:05:03,734
Fingerprints.

67
00:05:04,184 --> 00:05:14,934
For years, police in Europe had relied on a system invented in Paris by Alphonse Bair-tee-yon: careful measurements of a prisoner's head, arms, fingers and feet, written on a card.

68
00:05:14,934 --> 00:05:19,473
It worked, but it was slow, and it depended on the clerk with the callipers.

69
00:05:19,473 --> 00:05:21,026
Fingerprints were simpler.

70
00:05:21,026 --> 00:05:29,686
The ridges on a fingertip are formed before birth, and the evidence of the time was that they did not change, and that no two people shared them.

71
00:05:30,136 --> 00:05:33,793
The idea had several fathers, and they did not get on.

72
00:05:33,793 --> 00:05:46,051
A Scottish doctor, Henry Faulds, working as a missionary in Tokyo, had written to the journal Nature in 1880, suggesting that prints left at a crime scene could identify a criminal.

73
00:05:46,051 --> 00:05:56,278
A British official in India, William Herschel, replied that he had been using fingerprints for years to stop people cheating on contracts and pensions.

74
00:05:56,728 --> 00:06:02,775
The man who made it workable was Edward Henry, a senior police officer in Bengal.

75
00:06:02,775 --> 00:06:18,454
In the late 1890s, with two Indian sub-inspectors, Azizul Haque and Hemchandra Bose, he developed a way of sorting fingerprints into classes, so that one set could be found among thousands in minutes, not days.

76
00:06:18,903 --> 00:06:28,921
In 1901, Henry was brought to London to run the CID, and on the 1st of July that year he opened a Fingerprint Bureau at Scotland Yard.

77
00:06:28,921 --> 00:06:33,482
By 1905 he was Commissioner of the whole Metropolitan Police.

78
00:06:33,482 --> 00:06:43,425
The bureau had already helped convict a burglar, Harry Jackson, in 1902, from a print left on fresh paint at a house in south London.

79
00:06:43,875 --> 00:06:45,783
But a burglary was one thing.

80
00:06:45,783 --> 00:06:51,376
No one had ever been convicted of murder in Britain on the strength of a fingerprint.

81
00:06:51,376 --> 00:06:54,600
In a murder trial, a man's life would hang on it.

82
00:06:54,600 --> 00:07:00,588
The defence would attack it with everything they had, and the newspapers would be watching.

83
00:07:01,036 --> 00:07:06,303
When Mack-nawten heard about the empty cash box, he went to look at it himself.

84
00:07:06,303 --> 00:07:08,303
He turned the inner tray over.

85
00:07:08,303 --> 00:07:12,703
On its underside, on the smooth painted metal, there was a smudge.

86
00:07:12,703 --> 00:07:15,103
It looked like the mark of a finger.

87
00:07:15,555 --> 00:07:23,725
He picked up the box using his handkerchief, had it wrapped in paper, and sent it to the Fingerprint Bureau.

88
00:07:23,725 --> 00:07:27,508
It was the kind of care that later became routine.

89
00:07:27,508 --> 00:07:29,323
In 1905, it was unusual.

90
00:07:29,773 --> 00:07:38,598
The bureau was run by Detective Inspector Charles Stockley Collins, who was regarded as the leading fingerprint expert in the country.

91
00:07:38,598 --> 00:07:40,837
He studied the mark under a glass.

92
00:07:40,837 --> 00:07:44,328
It had not been made in blood or paint, but in sweat.

93
00:07:44,328 --> 00:07:47,423
It looked like a thumb, probably a right thumb.

94
00:07:47,875 --> 00:07:50,151
Collins began by ruling people out.

95
00:07:50,151 --> 00:07:53,858
He took the prints of the dead couple, and compared them.

96
00:07:53,858 --> 00:07:54,443
No match.

97
00:07:54,443 --> 00:08:00,166
Then he took the prints of Sergeant Atkinson, who had moved the box with his bare hands.

98
00:08:00,166 --> 00:08:02,377
That was the right thing to check.

99
00:08:02,377 --> 00:08:04,524
And the print was not his either.

100
00:08:04,973 --> 00:08:06,854
So it belonged to someone else.

101
00:08:06,854 --> 00:08:15,411
The bureau held somewhere between eighty and ninety thousand sets of prints, taken from people who had passed through the courts and prisons.

102
00:08:15,411 --> 00:08:17,050
Collins searched the files.

103
00:08:17,050 --> 00:08:18,203
There was no match.

104
00:08:18,203 --> 00:08:22,816
Whoever had left that thumbprint had never been fingerprinted by the police.

105
00:08:23,266 --> 00:08:25,314
The other hope was Ann Farrow.

106
00:08:25,314 --> 00:08:28,043
If she woke, she could describe the men.

107
00:08:28,043 --> 00:08:28,998
She never did.

108
00:08:28,998 --> 00:08:36,231
On the 31st of March, four days after she was found, she died in hospital without regaining consciousness.

109
00:08:36,231 --> 00:08:42,304
It was now a double murder, and the only person who had seen the killers' faces was gone.

110
00:08:42,754 --> 00:08:46,617
Scotland Yard had a print, but no name to put next to it.

111
00:08:46,617 --> 00:08:50,885
A fingerprint can tell a detective whether a suspect was there.

112
00:08:50,885 --> 00:08:54,002
On its own, it cannot tell him who to suspect.

113
00:08:54,002 --> 00:08:59,084
Could the old-fashioned business of knocking on doors find a name to match?

114
00:09:00,932 --> 00:09:01,524
It could.

115
00:09:01,524 --> 00:09:07,241
That Monday morning, Detford High Street had been waking up, and people had been about.

116
00:09:07,241 --> 00:09:15,127
Several witnesses said that at around half past seven, they had seen two men come out of the shop and walk quickly away.

117
00:09:15,578 --> 00:09:18,344
Their descriptions agreed on the clothes.

118
00:09:18,344 --> 00:09:21,110
One man wore a dark brown suit and a cap.

119
00:09:21,110 --> 00:09:24,820
The other wore a dark blue serge suit and a bowler hat.

120
00:09:24,820 --> 00:09:31,161
A milkman, Henry Jennings, had seen two men near the shop, but he could not say who they were.

121
00:09:31,610 --> 00:09:34,210
Two other witnesses were more certain.

122
00:09:34,210 --> 00:09:42,419
A man named Henry Littlefield and a young local woman named Ellen Stanton both said they knew the man in the brown suit.

123
00:09:42,419 --> 00:09:43,992
He was Alfred Stratton.

124
00:09:44,442 --> 00:09:48,610
Alfred Stratton was in his early twenties, born in 1882.

125
00:09:48,610 --> 00:09:55,160
He had no criminal record, but the local police knew him, and did not think well of him.

126
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:57,690
He did not hold down regular work.

127
00:09:57,690 --> 00:10:03,049
His younger brother, Albert, born in 1884, was also known to the police.

128
00:10:03,049 --> 00:10:08,110
The description of the second man, in the bowler hat, fitted Albert.

129
00:10:08,559 --> 00:10:12,687
The detectives went to see Alfred's girlfriend, Annie Cromarty.

130
00:10:12,687 --> 00:10:20,222
According to her statements, the day after the murders, Alfred had got rid of his brown coat and changed his shoes.

131
00:10:20,222 --> 00:10:24,546
And not long before, he had asked her for a pair of old stockings.

132
00:10:24,996 --> 00:10:27,638
Warrants were issued for both brothers.

133
00:10:27,638 --> 00:10:33,397
On Sunday, the 2nd of April, Alfred was arrested in a pub called the King of Prussia.

134
00:10:33,397 --> 00:10:37,055
The next day, Albert was picked up on the High Street.

135
00:10:37,055 --> 00:10:42,135
Both were taken to Greenwich police station on Blackheath Road and charged.

136
00:10:42,586 --> 00:10:49,773
And at the police station, both brothers had their fingerprints taken, in ink, on paper, the routine way.

137
00:10:49,773 --> 00:10:57,166
Annie Cromarty also led police to about four pounds in coins, which had been buried near a local waterworks.

138
00:10:57,615 --> 00:11:00,831
The inked cards went to Collins at Scotland Yard.

139
00:11:00,831 --> 00:11:05,424
He laid the mark from the cash box beside each finger of each brother.

140
00:11:05,424 --> 00:11:06,999
It did not match Albert.

141
00:11:06,999 --> 00:11:09,362
It did not match Alfred's left hand.

142
00:11:09,362 --> 00:11:11,789
Then he came to Alfred's right thumb.

143
00:11:12,240 --> 00:11:13,031
It matched.

144
00:11:13,031 --> 00:11:17,999
The same ridges, the same endings, the same forks in the same places.

145
00:11:17,999 --> 00:11:25,197
The cash box and the thumbprint card are still kept today, in the Crime Museum at New Scotland Yard.

146
00:11:25,648 --> 00:11:29,847
The brothers were charged with the murders of Thomas and Ann Farrow.

147
00:11:29,847 --> 00:11:32,997
But Mack-nawten and Collins understood the problem.

148
00:11:32,997 --> 00:11:35,159
The witnesses could be argued with.

149
00:11:35,159 --> 00:11:36,827
Nobody had seen the attack.

150
00:11:36,827 --> 00:11:45,473
The only piece of physical evidence that tied Alfred Stratton to the inside of that house was a mark of sweat, smaller than a postage stamp.

151
00:11:45,473 --> 00:11:47,635
The case would stand or fall on it.

152
00:11:47,635 --> 00:11:52,515
Would a jury in 1905 hang two men on a science most of them had never heard of?

153
00:11:54,365 --> 00:12:01,046
The trial opened at the Old Bailey on the 5th of May, 1905, before Mr Justice Channell.

154
00:12:01,046 --> 00:12:07,574
The case for the Crown was led by Richard Muir, the most feared prosecutor in London.

155
00:12:07,574 --> 00:12:11,568
Five years later he would also prosecute Dr Crippen.

156
00:12:12,019 --> 00:12:14,411
Muir was famous for his preparation.

157
00:12:14,411 --> 00:12:24,050
He wrote his questions on small cards in coloured pencil, one colour for each kind of question, and the lawyers called them Muir's playing cards.

158
00:12:24,050 --> 00:12:25,977
He trusted physical evidence.

159
00:12:25,977 --> 00:12:31,760
He distrusted eyewitnesses, unless their story fitted what the evidence already showed.

160
00:12:32,211 --> 00:12:34,402
He called more than forty witnesses.

161
00:12:34,402 --> 00:12:37,810
The people on the High Street, who had seen the two men.

162
00:12:37,810 --> 00:12:42,497
Kate Wade, Albert's girlfriend, who said he had not been with her that night.

163
00:12:42,497 --> 00:12:51,932
And Annie Cromarty, who said Alfred had come home that morning with money he did not explain, and had asked her to say that he had been with her all night.

164
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:57,338
The defence team knew where the real fight would be, and they had found two experts.

165
00:12:57,338 --> 00:13:03,181
One was Dr John Garson, a respected scientist who had championed the old Bair-tee-yon measurements.

166
00:13:03,181 --> 00:13:09,555
The other was Henry Faulds himself, the doctor who had first proposed using fingerprints to catch criminals.

167
00:13:09,555 --> 00:13:15,634
According to accounts of the case, Faulds argued that a single print was not enough to identify anyone.

168
00:13:15,634 --> 00:13:18,822
Hold on to that, because the defence never called him.

169
00:13:19,272 --> 00:13:22,403
The defence put Alfred Stratton into the witness box.

170
00:13:22,403 --> 00:13:29,849
His story was that, at about half past two that morning, Albert had tapped on his window, wanting money for a night's lodging.

171
00:13:29,849 --> 00:13:31,268
Alfred had none to give.

172
00:13:31,268 --> 00:13:37,768
He had found his brother in the street and let him sleep on his floor, and they had both stayed in until nine.

173
00:13:37,768 --> 00:13:42,555
The four pounds, he said, were winnings from a boxing match, buried weeks before.

174
00:13:43,004 --> 00:13:44,449
Muir had expected that.

175
00:13:44,449 --> 00:13:50,166
Before he called his fingerprint expert, he called a prison officer named William Gittings.

176
00:13:50,166 --> 00:14:00,407
Gittings told the court that, while the brothers were held awaiting trial, Albert had said to him: I reckon he will get strung up, and I shall get about ten years.

177
00:14:00,407 --> 00:14:01,914
He has led me into this.

178
00:14:02,365 --> 00:14:03,551
Then came Collins.

179
00:14:03,551 --> 00:14:10,407
Muir took him through the method slowly, in plain language, as if the jury had never seen a fingerprint.

180
00:14:10,407 --> 00:14:11,725
Most of them hadn't.

181
00:14:11,725 --> 00:14:13,901
Collins showed them the cash box.

182
00:14:13,901 --> 00:14:21,680
He showed them photographic enlargements of the mark from the tray and of Alfred Stratton's right thumb, side by side.

183
00:14:22,130 --> 00:14:28,070
He pointed out the places where the ridges matched: where a ridge ended, where one split in two.

184
00:14:28,070 --> 00:14:31,659
By his count, there were up to twelve points of agreement.

185
00:14:31,659 --> 00:14:43,600
When one of the jurors asked why the two images did not look exactly the same, Collins demonstrated how the same thumb leaves a different-looking mark when it is pressed harder or more lightly.

186
00:14:44,051 --> 00:14:46,322
Then the defence called Dr Garson.

187
00:14:46,322 --> 00:14:51,465
He had examined the same two prints, he said, and they were not in agreement.

188
00:14:51,465 --> 00:14:57,810
On paper, he was a heavyweight, and the defence presented him as one of Collins's own teachers.

189
00:14:57,810 --> 00:15:03,622
For a moment, it was expert against expert, and the jury had to decide whom to believe.

190
00:15:04,072 --> 00:15:07,249
There was one more thing the defence did not know.

191
00:15:07,249 --> 00:15:13,032
In cross-examination, Muir produced two letters, both written by Dr Garson on the same day.

192
00:15:13,032 --> 00:15:16,655
One was addressed to the Director of Public Prosecutions.

193
00:15:16,655 --> 00:15:19,642
The other was to the solicitor for the defence.

194
00:15:19,642 --> 00:15:24,980
Each, according to accounts of the trial, offered his services as an expert witness.

195
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:30,065
Muir asked him how he could reconcile writing those two letters on the same day.

196
00:15:30,514 --> 00:15:33,943
Garson answered: I am an independent witness.

197
00:15:33,943 --> 00:15:36,153
The judge was less impressed.

198
00:15:36,153 --> 00:15:45,371
Mr Justice Channell remarked that, after writing two such letters, Dr Garson was, in his words, absolutely untrustworthy.

199
00:15:45,821 --> 00:15:48,438
That was the end of the expert defence.

200
00:15:48,438 --> 00:15:54,276
Having watched Garson taken apart, the defence decided not to call Henry Faulds at all.

201
00:15:54,276 --> 00:16:02,128
The man who had first suggested that fingerprints could catch a killer never said a word at the trial that proved it.

202
00:16:03,979 --> 00:16:06,391
On the 6th of May, the jury retired.

203
00:16:06,391 --> 00:16:08,670
They were out for about two hours.

204
00:16:08,670 --> 00:16:14,100
They found both brothers guilty of murder, and the judge sentenced them to death.

205
00:16:14,548 --> 00:16:23,625
Alfred and Albert Stratton were hanged together at Wandsworth Prison, at nine o'clock in the morning, on the 23rd of May, 1905.

206
00:16:23,625 --> 00:16:30,557
Less than two months had passed since William Jones had knocked on a locked shop door in Detford.

207
00:16:31,008 --> 00:16:34,427
It is worth being precise about what the jury decided.

208
00:16:34,427 --> 00:16:37,276
They did not convict on the thumbprint alone.

209
00:16:37,276 --> 00:16:43,545
There were the identifications, the stockings, the clothes, the money and the remark in the prison.

210
00:16:43,545 --> 00:16:49,181
But the print was the only evidence that placed Alfred Stratton's hand inside that house.

211
00:16:49,181 --> 00:16:54,057
Without it, the case was a collection of things that could be explained away.

212
00:16:54,506 --> 00:17:00,440
After Detford, fingerprint evidence was no longer an experiment in a British murder court.

213
00:17:00,440 --> 00:17:06,900
Defence lawyers would keep attacking it, sometimes rightly, on how a print was lifted or compared.

214
00:17:06,900 --> 00:17:12,372
But the principle had been tested in the hardest setting there is, and it had held.

215
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:23,626
The Henry system, built in Bengal with the work of Azizul Haque and Hemchandra Bose, spread through police forces across the English-speaking world and beyond.

216
00:17:23,626 --> 00:17:27,975
For most of the twentieth century, it was how prints were filed.

217
00:17:28,426 --> 00:17:31,245
Some of the same people would meet again.

218
00:17:31,245 --> 00:17:38,600
Five years later, Mack-nawten was overseeing the hunt for Dr Crippen, and Richard Muir was prosecuting him.

219
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:41,556
That case has its own file on this channel.

220
00:17:42,005 --> 00:17:49,695
Henry Faulds spent the rest of his life arguing that he had never received the credit he deserved for the idea.

221
00:17:49,695 --> 00:17:53,575
He died in Staffordshire in 1930, still bitter about it.

222
00:17:53,575 --> 00:18:00,019
Today there are plaques in his memory, and a memorial in his home town of Beith, in Ayrshire.

223
00:18:00,469 --> 00:18:10,127
The Detford murders are remembered for one first: the first time a British jury convicted anyone of murder with a fingerprint at the heart of the case.

224
00:18:10,127 --> 00:18:22,471
It came down to a sergeant's bare hands that did not spoil it, an assistant commissioner's handkerchief that protected it, and a detective inspector who could explain it to twelve ordinary men.

225
00:18:22,923 --> 00:18:25,558
But this case begins with two people.

226
00:18:25,558 --> 00:18:37,312
Thomas Farrow, who was 71, and Ann Farrow, who was 65, who ran a paint shop on Detford High Street and kept the takings safe for their employer until Monday morning.

227
00:18:37,312 --> 00:18:40,019
They are remembered here with respect.

228
00:18:40,469 --> 00:18:42,494
This case file is closed.

229
00:18:42,494 --> 00:18:46,707
New case files arrive every day on Precinct Archive.
